-- Mighty Wash Academy: the Maintenance track (T1 to T3), written from the
-- "Maintenance Education" document (Manager Basic Knowledge). Run once with the service
-- role after migration 0223; academy__import refuses a track that already has levels.
-- It also makes General Manager (l6) require T1 Maintenance.
-- After this, edit the content in Training > Edit content.

select public.academy__import('54f3e299-1f61-4ed2-9921-3d02160b72e6', $academy${"track":{"key":"maintenance","title":"Maintenance","unit_label":"Tier","code_prefix":"T","sort":2,"eyebrow":"Maintenance certification","headline":"Know it. Fix it. Teach it.","intro":"Three tiers of maintenance certification. Tier 1 is knowing how the wash works and what to check when it doesn't. Tier 2 is doing the site-level work: identifying issues, hoses, tools, minor electrical and oils. Tier 3 is teaching it and talking team members through it. A General Manager is certified only once they hold Tier 1.","why":"A wash that is down isn't washing cars or keeping members happy. Most stops are something a trained leader on site can find in minutes. The leader who knows the systems gets the tunnel running again, or hands the service tech a clear answer instead of \"it stopped.\""},"levels":[{"key":"t1","num":1,"title":"Tier 1: Maintenance Basic Knowledge","short":"T1 Maintenance","draft":true,"pass":80,"role":"Tier 1 is the maintenance knowledge every manager needs: what each system does, where it is, how to tell it is working, the first checks to make when it is not, and when to call the service tech. A General Manager is not certified until they hold Tier 1.","blurb":"How the wash works, system by system, with the first checks for each and what to do when the wash or a brush goes down.","why":"When the wash stops, cars back up and the crew looks to the manager. Most stops are something a trained manager can find in minutes: an E-stop left engaged, a roller jam, low air, a tripped breaker. Knowing the systems is what turns \"it stopped\" into a fix, or into a clear call to the service tech.","ack_statement":"I know what each of the wash systems does and where it is, how to tell when each one is working correctly, the first checks to make when one is not, the site-level maintenance each one needs, and when to stop and call the service tech.","skills":[],"requires":[],"courses":[{"title":"Maintenance Basic Knowledge","lessons":[{"key":"t1-1","title":"How the Wash Works","minutes":5,"why":"When the wash stops, the clock starts. Knowing what each system does and where it lives lets you go straight to the right place instead of guessing.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The wash works like a human body. Every system has a job, and when one stops, the whole wash feels it. Learn the body and you can find the problem fast.</p>\n<h2>The body of the wash</h2>\n<table><tr><th>System</th><th>Its job</th><th>Where it is</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">MCC</td><td>The brain. Tells the wash what to do when a car is sent.</td><td>Chemical room</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Pulse</td><td>The heart. Keeps track of wash measurements and car lengths.</td><td>End of the bay, on the conveyor drive</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Air compressors</td><td>The lungs. Keep pressure on the brushes, chain tension and chemical panels.</td><td>Chemical room</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Photo eyes and high gate</td><td>The eyes. Tell the MCC a vehicle is entering and start measuring it.</td><td>Wash start, right before the presoak arch</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Conveyor</td><td>The backbone. Its rollers push each vehicle through and keep it straight.</td><td>All the way down the wash</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Brushes</td><td>The hands and feet. Friction that cleans the vehicle.</td><td>Through the tunnel</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Blowers</td><td>Remove water with high-pressure air before the car exits.</td><td>End of the wash bay</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Vacuums</td><td>The customer vacs.</td><td>Under the canopy</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Prep unit</td><td>Removes bugs and mud before the tunnel.</td><td>Prep</td></tr></table>\n<h2>The three tiers</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Tier 1:</strong> know this material.</li><li><strong>Tier 2:</strong> capable of doing the work: identifying issues, recognizing hose sizes, working with tools, changing hoses, minor electrical and changing oils.</li><li><strong>Tier 3:</strong> Tier 1 and Tier 2, plus teaching the items and talking team members through them.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"eyebrow red\">Required for General Managers</div><p>A General Manager can't be certified until they hold Tier 1.</p></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Which system is the brain that tells the wash what to do when a car is sent?","o":["Pulse","MCC","Air compressors","Photo eyes"],"a":1,"why":"The MCC is the brain. It is in the chemical room."},{"q":"Where are the MCC and the air compressors?","o":["Under the canopy","At the end of the bay","In the chemical room","At the presoak arch"],"a":2,"why":"Both are in the chemical room."},{"q":"What does the pulse keep track of?","o":["Chemical levels","Air pressure","Vacuum suction","Wash measurements and car lengths"],"a":3,"why":"The pulse is the heart: it keeps track of wash measurements and car lengths."},{"q":"Which system is the backbone that pushes vehicles through and keeps them straight?","o":["Conveyor","Blowers","High gate","Prep unit"],"a":0,"why":"The conveyor runs all the way down the wash. Its rollers push the vehicle."},{"q":"What does Tier 3 add to Tier 1 and Tier 2?","o":["Rebuilding motors","Ordering parts","Teaching the items and talking team members through them","Nothing"],"a":2,"why":"Tier 3 is the teacher: able to teach the items and talk a team member through them."}]},{"key":"t1-2","title":"The MCC: The Brain","minutes":5,"why":"Almost every stop leaves a trace in the MCC. Going there first turns \"the wash stopped\" into a specific cause you can fix or hand to the service tech.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The MCC is the brain of the operation. It is in the chemical room, and it tells the wash what to do when a car is sent.</p>\n<h2>Check for errors when the site is having issues</h2>\n<p>The MCC's alerts, history and I/O pages show what stopped and why. Look there for:</p>\n<ul><li>Pulse failures and timing</li><li>E-stop issues</li><li>Chain tension</li><li>Photo eye and high gate</li><li>Most equipment failures</li></ul>\n<h2>Turn equipment on and off by hand</h2>\n<ul><li>Test chemical</li><li>Test tunnel equipment</li><li>Shut off equipment if it malfunctions</li></ul>\n<h2>The power source to the wash equipment</h2>\n<ul><li>Find tripped breakers that cause a machine to malfunction</li><li>Check for tripped VFDs</li><li>Check that the equipment is working</li></ul>\n<div class=\"callout blue\"><div class=\"eyebrow blue\">Reading a VFD</div><p>A VFD that is working correctly reads <strong>0000</strong>. A VFD only shows letters when there is an error.</p></div>","quiz":[{"q":"Where is the MCC?","o":["In the chemical room","At the end of the bay","Under the canopy","In the office"],"a":0,"why":"The MCC is in the chemical room."},{"q":"Which of these can you check for in the MCC when the site is having issues?","o":["Pulse failures","E-stop issues","Chain tension","All of these"],"a":3,"why":"Pulse, E-stops, chain tension, photo eyes and most equipment failures all show in the MCC."},{"q":"A VFD that is working correctly reads ...","o":["P5","0000","ERR","Letters"],"a":1,"why":"0000 means working. Letters mean an error."},{"q":"What can you do with the MCC by hand?","o":["Change wash prices","Adjust vacuum suction","Reset the pay station","Test chemical and tunnel equipment, and shut off equipment that malfunctions"],"a":3,"why":"Manual control is for testing and for shutting off a malfunction."},{"q":"A machine is not working. Which MCC check is part of Tier 1?","o":["Rewire the motor","Look for a tripped breaker or a tripped VFD","Replace the controller","Nothing; call right away"],"a":1,"why":"Tripped breakers and VFDs are the first power checks."}]},{"key":"t1-3","title":"The Pulse: The Heart","minutes":4,"why":"Every brush and chemical is timed off the pulse. When it is off, the wash can look like it is running while it soaps cars late or misses them, and the customer sees it before you do.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The pulse is the heart of the operation. It sits at the end of the bay on the conveyor drive and spins with it, keeping track of wash measurements and car lengths.</p>\n<h2>When the pulse malfunctions, the timing is off</h2>\n<ul><li>Soap turns on late.</li><li>Cars come out with soap on them, or not washed correctly.</li><li>The wash stops on a pulse failure.</li></ul>\n<h2>Troubleshooting a pulse failure</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Check that the puck is spinning.</li><li>Look for a bad wire.</li><li>Look for a busted hose.</li><li>Go to the MCC error page.</li><li>Check the pulse reading on the MCC's I/O page.</li></ol>","quiz":[{"q":"Where is the pulse?","o":["In the chemical room","At the end of the bay on the conveyor drive","At the presoak arch","Under the canopy"],"a":1,"why":"It spins with the conveyor drive at the end of the bay."},{"q":"Soap is turning on late and cars come out soapy. Which system is the likely cause?","o":["Pulse","Vacuums","Blowers","Prep unit"],"a":0,"why":"Timing problems point to the pulse."},{"q":"What is the first thing to look at on a pulse failure?","o":["The vac socks","The blower nets","Whether the puck is spinning","The tire shine"],"a":2,"why":"Start at the puck: is it spinning?"},{"q":"Where in the MCC do you check the pulse reading?","o":["The history of chemical orders","The I/O page","The price page","The vacuum page"],"a":1,"why":"The pulse reading is on the I/O page."},{"q":"Which of these can cause a pulse failure?","o":["A bad wire","A busted hose","A puck that is not spinning","Any of these"],"a":3,"why":"Puck, wire and hose are the three to check."}]},{"key":"t1-4","title":"Air Compressors: The Lungs","minutes":5,"why":"Low air shows up all over the tunnel at once: sagging brushes, weak soap, a chain that will not stay on. Knowing those signs point back to the compressors saves you from chasing five problems that are really one.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The air compressors are the lungs of the wash. They are in the chemical room and keep pressure on the brush extends and retracts, the chain tension and the chemical panels.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"eyebrow red\">The standard</div><p>All air compressor pressure should be at <strong>125 PSI</strong> before you turn on equipment or wash cars.</p></div>\n<h2>What the air does</h2>\n<ul><li>Helps brushes come in and out</li><li>Creates agitation for the soaps</li><li>Maintains chain tension</li><li>Brings the rollers up</li></ul>\n<h2>Signs of an air problem</h2>\n<ul><li>Sagging top brushes, or brushes not coming in correctly</li><li>Soap comes out weak</li><li>The chain keeps coming off the sprocket</li><li>Rollers won't come up</li></ul>\n<h2>Air compressor maintenance</h2>\n<table><tr><th>How often</th><th>What</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Daily</td><td>Drain the water</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Weekly</td><td>Check the air filters. Check the belt tension.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Monthly</td><td>Change the oil</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Every check</td><td>Look for oil around the pumps</td></tr></table>","quiz":[{"q":"What should air compressor pressure be before you turn on equipment or wash cars?","o":["90 PSI","100 PSI","125 PSI","150 PSI"],"a":2,"why":"125 PSI before equipment goes on."},{"q":"Which of these is a sign of an air problem?","o":["Sagging top brushes","Weak soap","Rollers will not come up","All of these"],"a":3,"why":"Air runs the brushes, the soap agitation, chain tension and the rollers."},{"q":"How often do you drain the water from the air compressors?","o":["Daily","Weekly","Monthly","Yearly"],"a":0,"why":"Drain water daily."},{"q":"How often is the air compressor oil changed?","o":["Daily","Weekly","Every 3 months","Monthly"],"a":3,"why":"Oil monthly."},{"q":"Air filters and belt tension are checked ...","o":["weekly","daily","monthly","when something breaks"],"a":0,"why":"Both are weekly checks."}]},{"key":"t1-5","title":"Photo Eyes and the High Gate","minutes":5,"why":"The moment a car crosses the beam, the wash starts measuring it, and that measurement decides when every brush and chemical turns on and off. If the eyes cannot see, the wash cannot time the car.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The photo eyes and high gate eyes signal the MCC that a vehicle is entering the wash and start the measuring. They are at the wash start, right before the presoak arch.</p>\n<p>As soon as a car crosses the photo eye beam, the wash starts taking its length measurement, which tells the wash when brushes, chemicals and the rest turn on and off. The high gate tells whether the vehicle is a car or a truck. Its only job is to abort the top brush on an open bed and close the power locks on the blowers.</p>\n<h2>Working correctly</h2>\n<ul><li>The lights on both eyes are on.</li><li>The yellow light turns on when the eye is crossed.</li><li>The light on the MCC's I/O page turns green when the eye is crossed.</li><li>Chemicals and brushes turn on when called for as the car enters.</li></ul>\n<h2>Troubleshooting</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><strong>Check the eyes are lined up.</strong> When they are lined up, the transmitter shows its light. When they are out of line, the yellow light stays on as if a car were crossing the beam.</li>\n<li><strong>Check the MCC.</strong> Open the I/O tab and find the photo eye under inputs. The green light should turn on when the beam is crossed.</li>\n<li><strong>No lights on the photo eyes at all:</strong> open the conduit box and make sure there is no water in it, open the MCC doors and make sure no small breakers are tripped, then call your service tech.</li></ol>","quiz":[{"q":"Where are the photo eyes?","o":["At the blowers","At the wash start, right before the presoak arch","In the chemical room","At the vacuums"],"a":1,"why":"They sit at the wash start, before presoak."},{"q":"What is the high gate's only function?","o":["Measure car length","Turn on presoak","Abort the top brush on an open bed and close the power locks on the blowers","Count cars"],"a":2,"why":"It tells car from truck: top brush abort on an open bed, blower power locks."},{"q":"The yellow light on a photo eye stays on with no car in the beam. What is most likely?","o":["The eyes are out of line","The pulse failed","A blower tripped","Low vacuum suction"],"a":0,"why":"Out-of-line eyes read as a car crossing."},{"q":"Neither photo eye has any lights on. What do you check?","o":["The vac socks","The prep pump oil","The tire shine","Water in the conduit box, then small breakers in the MCC, then call the service tech"],"a":3,"why":"Water and small breakers first, then the service tech."},{"q":"On the MCC I/O page, what happens when a car crosses the beam?","o":["The input light turns green","The screen goes blank","The VFD reads P5","Nothing"],"a":0,"why":"Green on the I/O page means the MCC saw the car."}]},{"key":"t1-6","title":"The Conveyor: The Backbone","minutes":6,"why":"A stopped conveyor stops everything. Most conveyor stops are something on the lot, like a roller jam, an E-stop left engaged or a car sitting at the anti-collision, and you can clear them in minutes if you know where to look.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The conveyor is the backbone of the wash, running all the way down the tunnel. The rollers sit in it, push each vehicle through and keep it moving straight. It is powered by either an electric motor or hydraulics.</p>\n<h2>Working correctly</h2>\n<ul><li>The chain runs smooth, with no skipping.</li><li>Rollers come up on command.</li><li>No roller jams.</li><li>The chain keeps moving.</li><li>No sagging chain links.</li><li>The chain tension cylinders are not fully extended.</li><li>The chain tension regulator holds a steady <strong>90 PSI</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2>When the chain stops</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Check</th><th>How</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Roller jams</td><td>Walk down the track and make sure every roller is straight, not twisted up. Look at the roller-up and roller-down areas for jams.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">E-stops and anti-collision</td><td>Push and pull every E-stop. Clear the exit end of the bay of any car sitting at the anti-collision. On the MCC, check the history and alerts tabs, and the I/O page to make sure no E-stop or the anti-collision is engaged.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Pulse failure</td><td>Check the I/O page, and the pulse hose and switch at the end of the bay. Then work through the pulse checks.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Electric drive</td><td>Open the MCC doors and check whether the VFD tripped or shows an error code. It should read 0000.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Hydraulic drive</td><td>Look for oil spills around the area, check the hydraulic packs are filled with oil, and check whether the hydraulic motor breaker tripped.</td></tr></table>\n<h2>When the chain slips off the sprocket</h2>\n<ul><li>Look for roller jams.</li><li>Check the air compressor pressure. If it is below 90 PSI, look for air leaks and make sure the compressors are turning on.</li><li>Chain tension pressure should be 90 PSI. If it is lower, check the air compressor.</li><li>Chain tension cylinders <strong>fully extended</strong>: a link needs to be removed.</li><li>Chain tension cylinders <strong>fully collapsed</strong>: check the air tension pressure and the air compressors.</li></ul>\n<h2>Site-level maintenance</h2>\n<ul><li>Grease the take-up bearings.</li><li>Remove a chain link when the chain is loose.</li><li>Change rollers.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"What should the chain tension regulator read?","o":["125 PSI","A steady 90 PSI","50 PSI","It changes all day"],"a":1,"why":"A consistent 90 PSI."},{"q":"The chain stopped. Which first checks are on the lot?","o":["Change the hydraulic oil","Replace the pulse","Walk the track for roller jams and push and pull every E-stop","Call corporate"],"a":2,"why":"Jams and E-stops are the common, quick causes."},{"q":"A car is sitting at the exit end of the bay and the conveyor will not run. What is likely engaged?","o":["The anti-collision","The high gate","The tire shine","The prep unit"],"a":0,"why":"Clear the exit end of the bay of any car at the anti-collision."},{"q":"The chain tension cylinders are fully extended. What does that mean?","o":["The air is too high","The pulse failed","Nothing","A link needs to be removed"],"a":3,"why":"Fully extended means the chain is loose: a link comes out."},{"q":"On an electric drive, where do you look when the conveyor will not run?","o":["The VFD in the MCC, for a trip or an error code","The vacuum turbine","The blower nets","The prep tank"],"a":0,"why":"The drive VFD should read 0000."}]},{"key":"t1-7","title":"Brushes: The Hands and Feet","minutes":5,"why":"Brushes are where a mechanical problem turns into a damaged or dirty car. A brush that will not come in, will not spin, or will not move out of the way is a problem you stop for, not one you wash through.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The 701, 300, 301, wraps, tire brushes and tire shine are friction equipment: brushes that apply friction to clean vehicles. They all have bearings that need to be greased and air cylinders that need to be maintained.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Brush</th><th>What it does</th><th>Moves by</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">701 and wraps (6 ft tall)</td><td>Cleans the front, side and back. Its mirror bump feature lets the brush move out when it senses a rear-view mirror.</td><td>Air cylinder</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">300 and 301 (35 in. tall)</td><td>Cleans the sides and running boards.</td><td>Air cylinder</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Tire brush and tire shine</td><td>Cleans tires and wheels, and shines the tires.</td><td>Air cylinder</td></tr></table>\n<h2>Troubleshooting</h2>\n<p><strong>Brushes not moving in or out</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Check the air pressure at the air compressor and at the brush assembly.</li><li>Check for leaks around the air cylinder, then call the service tech.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>A brush not spinning or not turning on</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Check the MCC alerts page for errors.</li><li>Open the MCC and check the VFD for error codes. A VFD only shows letters when there is an error.</li><li>If the brush is hydraulic, look for leaks around the brush and the hydraulic pack, and notify your service tech.</li></ul>\n<h2>Site-level maintenance</h2>\n<ul><li>Grease the bearings.</li><li>Grease the cylinder shafts.</li><li>Change fittings if needed.</li><li>Drain and add oil to the air water separators.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"How tall is the 701 or wrap brush?","o":["35 inches","4 feet","6 feet","10 feet"],"a":2,"why":"The 701 and wraps are 6 ft tall. The 300 and 301 are 35 inches."},{"q":"What lets a wrap move out of the way of a rear-view mirror?","o":["The high gate","The pulse","The anti-collision","The mirror bump feature"],"a":3,"why":"Mirror bump moves the brush out when it senses a mirror."},{"q":"Brushes will not move in or out. What do you check first?","o":["Air pressure at the compressor and the brush assembly","The VFD for P5","The vac socks","The prep gun"],"a":0,"why":"Brushes move by air cylinder, so start with air."},{"q":"A brush is not spinning. Where do you look for the error?","o":["The pay station","The MCC alerts page and the VFD","The blower nets","The photo eyes"],"a":1,"why":"Alerts page first, then the VFD for letters."},{"q":"Which is site-level brush maintenance?","o":["Rebuilding the motor","Grease bearings and cylinder shafts, change fittings if needed, drain and oil the air water separators","Nothing; it is all the service tech","Replacing the VFD"],"a":1,"why":"Grease, fittings and the air water separators."}]},{"key":"t1-8","title":"Blowers, Vacuums and the Prep Unit","minutes":6,"why":"A car that leaves wet, a vacuum with no suction and a prep gun that will not spray are complaints that happen in front of the customer. Most of the fixes are site-level.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Three more systems the customer feels right away: the blowers at the end of the wash bay, the vacuums under the canopy, and the prep unit that takes bugs and mud off before the tunnel.</p>\n<h2>Blowers</h2>\n<p>The blowers use high-pressure air to remove excess water before the vehicle exits.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Air not coming out with high force:</strong> check the power lock is opening, check there is no trash in the blower net, and check the air pressure.</li>\n<li><strong>Blower not spinning:</strong> check the MCC for alerts, open the MCC to see if the blower tripped, and notify your service tech.</li>\n<li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> clear the blower nets, grease the power lock, wipe down the housing.</li></ul>\n<h2>Vacuums</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>One or two bays clogged:</strong> remove the cap and run a drain snake.</li>\n<li><strong>All bays low suction:</strong> open the canisters and check the vac socks are hung correctly, check the canister screen, and check for debris in the socks.</li>\n<li><strong>Turbine not spinning:</strong> check the VFD for errors, check whether a breaker tripped, and call your service tech.</li></ul>\n<table><tr><th>How often</th><th>Vacuum maintenance</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Daily</td><td>Shake the socks and clear the bin. Wipe down the seals. Clear the canister screen.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Weekly</td><td>Lube the hinges.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Every 3 months</td><td>Remove the socks, pressure wash them and let them dry.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Regularly</td><td>Grease the turbine bearings.</td></tr></table>\n<h2>Prep unit</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Low pump pressure:</strong> check the chemical filter in the prep tank, check the tank has water, check the nozzle for clogs, and check the hoses and unit for leaks.</li>\n<li><strong>Leaks from the pump to the prep gun:</strong> a leaking swivel or a busted hose (both are Tier 2 fixes).</li>\n<li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> change the prep gun when it leaks from the handle or stops working, change the pump oil monthly, and each week check the belt tension, clean around the area and check for oil leaks.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"Blower air is weak. Which of these is NOT one of the checks?","o":["The power lock is opening","No trash in the blower net","Air pressure","The vac socks"],"a":3,"why":"Power lock, net and air are the blower checks."},{"q":"Only one or two vacuum bays are clogged. What is the fix?","o":["Remove the cap and run a drain snake","Change the turbine","Pressure wash every sock","Call the service tech"],"a":0,"why":"A clog in one or two bays is in the line: snake it."},{"q":"All vacuum bays have low suction. Where do you look?","o":["The blowers","The pulse","The canisters: socks hung right, canister screen, debris in the socks","The prep tank"],"a":2,"why":"Every bay weak points to the canisters."},{"q":"How often are vac socks removed, pressure washed and dried?","o":["Daily","Every 3 months","Weekly","Monthly"],"a":1,"why":"Every 3 months. Shake them daily."},{"q":"The prep gun has low pressure. Which is a first check?","o":["The MCC VFDs","The high gate","The anti-collision","The chemical filter in the prep tank"],"a":3,"why":"Filter, water, nozzle, leaks."}]},{"key":"t1-9","title":"Fire Drills: When the Wash Goes Down","minutes":4,"why":"A down wash with a calm, clear manager is a short stop. Without one, the crew stands around, cars pile up and the service tech gets a vague call. The order below keeps everyone moving.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">When the wash goes down, the crew takes its cue from you. A fire drill is the order you work in, so the problem gets found and the lot stays under control.</p>\n<h2>The wash goes down, for any reason</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Find the issue.</li><li>Inform the crew.</li><li>Direct the crew.</li><li>Start the corrective operation if possible.</li><li>Call for service tech help.</li></ol>\n<h2>A brush goes down</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Find the issue.</li><li>Troubleshoot it.</li><li>Inform the service tech.</li><li>Know how to tie the brush back if needed.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"callout blue\"><div class=\"eyebrow blue\">Calling the service tech</div><p>Give them what you found: the MCC alert or code, what you saw, what you checked and tried, and since when. A photo helps.</p></div>","quiz":[{"q":"The wash goes down. What is the manager's first step?","o":["Call corporate","Send the crew home","Find the issue","Restart everything"],"a":2,"why":"Find the issue first, then inform and direct the crew."},{"q":"After finding the issue, what comes next?","o":["Inform and direct the crew","Leave the site","Wait for it to fix itself","Close the vacuums"],"a":0,"why":"The crew needs to know what is happening and what to do."},{"q":"A brush goes down. Which is part of the manager's job?","o":["Remove the brush","Know how to tie the brush back if needed","Keep washing through it","Ignore it until closing"],"a":1,"why":"Find it, troubleshoot, inform the service tech, tie it back if needed."},{"q":"When you call the service tech, what do you give them?","o":["Just \"it stopped\"","The car count","Nothing; they will figure it out","The MCC alert, what you saw, what you checked and tried, and since when"],"a":3,"why":"A clear call gets a faster fix."}]}]}],"scenarios":[{"t":"The Saturday stop","s":"The conveyor stops mid-morning on a busy Saturday. The MCC alerts tab shows an E-stop. The crew says nobody touched anything.","o":["Call the service tech and wait","Restart the conveyor over and over until it runs","Push and pull every E-stop, confirm on the I/O page that none is engaged, then restart","Close the wash for the day"],"a":2,"why":"An engaged E-stop is a site-level fix. Find it, clear it, confirm on the I/O page."},{"t":"Soap at the exit","s":"Cars are coming out with soap on them and the presoak seems to start late. The brushes and chemicals are all running.","o":["Check the pulse: puck spinning, wire, hose, the MCC error page and the pulse reading on the I/O page","Turn up the chemical","Replace the brushes","Tell the crew to rinse cars by hand"],"a":0,"why":"Late soap and soapy cars are timing problems, and timing comes from the pulse."},{"t":"Opening with a sagging brush","s":"At opening, the top brush is sagging and a test of the soap looks weak. No cars have been washed yet.","o":["Start washing; it will come up once it warms up","Call the chemical vendor","Turn off the top brush for the day","Check the air compressors are at 125 PSI before washing, and look for air leaks if they are not"],"a":3,"why":"Sagging brushes and weak soap together point to air. 125 PSI before equipment goes on."},{"t":"No lights, no trigger","s":"Cars are not triggering the wash. Neither photo eye has a light on.","o":["Wipe the eyes and keep sending cars","Check the conduit box for water and the MCC for tripped small breakers, then call the service tech","Change the pulse","Unplug the MCC and plug it back in"],"a":1,"why":"No lights at all means no power to the eyes: water in the conduit box or a tripped small breaker."},{"t":"The open bed","s":"An open-bed pickup goes through and the top brush comes down into the bed instead of aborting.","o":["Nothing is wrong; it happens","Slow the conveyor","Stop and check the high gate eyes: lined up, lights on, and showing on the MCC I/O page, then tell your service tech","Turn off the blowers"],"a":2,"why":"Aborting the top brush on an open bed is the high gate's job. If it did not, the high gate did not see the truck."}],"assessment":[{"q":"The MCC is ...","o":["the heart, at the end of the bay","the brain, in the chemical room","the lungs, under the canopy","the eyes, at the presoak arch"],"a":1},{"q":"The pulse is located ...","o":["at the end of the bay on the conveyor drive","in the chemical room","at the blowers","at the prep station"],"a":0},{"q":"Air compressor pressure before turning on equipment or washing cars:","o":["90 PSI","100 PSI","120 PSI","125 PSI"],"a":3},{"q":"Chain tension regulator pressure:","o":["A steady 90 PSI","125 PSI","60 PSI","Whatever it reads"],"a":0},{"q":"A working VFD reads ...","o":["letters","P5","0000","nothing"],"a":2},{"q":"The high gate's only function is to ...","o":["measure car length","abort the top brush on an open bed and close the blower power locks","start presoak","count cars"],"a":1},{"q":"Chain tension cylinders fully extended means ...","o":["the air is too high","a link needs to be removed","the pulse failed","all is well"],"a":1},{"q":"All vacuum bays have low suction. Check ...","o":["the socks, canister screen and debris in the canisters","the blower nets","the photo eyes","the pulse"],"a":0},{"q":"Air compressor oil is changed ...","o":["daily","weekly","monthly","yearly"],"a":2},{"q":"Brushes will not move in or out. Start with ...","o":["the VFD","the vac socks","the prep tank","air pressure at the compressor and the brush assembly"],"a":3},{"q":"The wash goes down. The manager's order is ...","o":["call the tech, then look","find the issue, inform the crew, direct the crew, start the corrective operation if possible, call for service tech help","send the crew to the vacuums and wait","restart until it runs"],"a":1},{"q":"No lights on either photo eye. Check ...","o":["water in the conduit box and small breakers in the MCC, then call the service tech","the tire shine","the wrap brush","the blower power lock"],"a":0}]},{"key":"t2","num":2,"title":"Tier 2: Maintenance Hands-On","short":"T2 Maintenance","draft":true,"pass":80,"role":"Tier 2 is being capable of the site-level work: identifying issues, recognizing hose sizes, working with tools, changing hoses, minor electrical and changing oils. It builds on Tier 1, and a leader signs off each hands-on skill after watching it done.","blurb":"Finding the issue fast, hoses and fittings, minor electrical, oils and grease, the conveyor chain, and the site maintenance schedule. Ends with hands-on skills a leader checks off.","why":"Knowing what a system does is Tier 1. Getting the wash back up is Tier 2. Every hose, oil change and breaker reset a site leader can do safely is a service call the site does not wait on.","ack_statement":"I have completed Tier 2 Maintenance. I lock out before I put a hand on equipment, I can identify which system stopped the wash, recognize and match hose sizes, use the right tools, change hoses and fittings, handle minor electrical resets, change oils and grease the equipment, and keep the site maintenance schedule. I call the service tech for anything past site level.","skills":["Identify the issue: use the MCC alerts, history and I/O pages to find which system stopped the wash","Lock out and tag out equipment before hands-on work, and verify it will not start","Recognize the hose sizes on site and match a replacement to the hose removed","Use the right tools: two crescent wrenches on a swivel, a grease gun, a drain snake","Change a prep gun swivel or hose and re-Teflon the fitting","Minor electrical: find and reset a tripped breaker, read a VFD and clear a P5","Change the oil in an air compressor and in the prep pump","Grease the brush bearings and cylinder shafts, the take-up bearings and the blower power lock","Remove a loose conveyor chain link and record it in the RTC"],"requires":["t1"],"courses":[{"title":"Maintenance Hands-On","lessons":[{"key":"t2-1","title":"Safety and Tools Before You Touch Anything","minutes":5,"why":"Tier 2 is the first tier that puts your hands on the equipment. The tunnel starts with no warning, holds air under pressure and runs on power that can hurt you. The steps below come before every fix, every time.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Before any hands-on work, the equipment is stopped, locked out and verified. Then the right tool for the job.</p>\n<h2>Lockout first, every time</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Stop the equipment at the control and tell the lot you are working on it.</li><li>Disconnect at the equipment's disconnect switch, not just the control panel.</li><li>Tag it with your name and the time so nobody restarts it.</li><li>Verify it will not start before you put a hand on anything.</li><li>Clear and restart only after your tools and hands are out and you have removed your own tag.</li></ol>\n<h2>One reset</h2>\n<p>A tripped breaker or a fault gets one reset. If it trips again or the same fault returns, stop resetting and call it in. Repeated resets hide a failing part until it fails for good.</p>\n<h2>Working with tools</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Used for</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Two crescent wrenches</td><td>Swivels and fittings: one holds, one turns, so you don't twist the hose or the line</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Teflon tape</td><td>Re-sealing threaded fittings when they go back on</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Grease gun</td><td>Bearings, cylinder shafts, take-up bearings, power locks</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Drain snake</td><td>A clogged vacuum line at one or two bays</td></tr></table>","quiz":[{"q":"Before putting a hand on equipment, what comes first?","o":["Grab the right wrench","Stop it, disconnect it, tag it and verify it will not start","Tell the service tech","Turn the conveyor slower"],"a":1,"why":"Lockout first, every time."},{"q":"Where do you disconnect the equipment?","o":["At the equipment's disconnect switch, not just the control panel","At the pay station","At the photo eyes","Only at the control panel"],"a":0,"why":"The control panel alone is not a lockout."},{"q":"A breaker trips again right after you reset it. What now?","o":["Reset it again","Tape it on","Stop resetting and call it in","Ignore it"],"a":2,"why":"One reset. A second trip is a failing part."},{"q":"Why use two crescent wrenches on a swivel?","o":["It is faster","One holds and one turns, so the hose or line does not twist","They are the only tools on site","To loosen it more"],"a":1,"why":"Hold and turn."},{"q":"Who removes the lockout tag?","o":["Whoever is closest","The next shift","The service tech only","The person who put it on, once tools and hands are out"],"a":3,"why":"Your tag, your removal."}]},{"key":"t2-2","title":"Identifying the Issue","minutes":6,"why":"Most of the time lost in a breakdown is spent looking in the wrong place. A fixed order, MCC first and then the system it points to, gets to the cause fast and gives the service tech a real answer when it is past site level.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Identifying issues is the first Tier 2 skill: get from \"the wash stopped\" to the system and the cause.</p>\n<h2>The order</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><strong>MCC first.</strong> Read the alerts tab, then the history tab, then the I/O page.</li><li><strong>Match the symptom to the system</strong> using the table below.</li><li><strong>Check it on the lot.</strong> Look, listen, and work through that system's checks.</li><li><strong>Fix it at site level</strong> if it is a Tier 2 fix, after lockout.</li><li><strong>Otherwise call the service tech</strong> with the alert, what you saw, what you checked and tried, and since when.</li></ol>\n<h2>Symptom to system</h2>\n<table><tr><th>What you see</th><th>System</th><th>First checks</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Soap late, cars soapy or missed, pulse failure alert</td><td class=\"k\">Pulse</td><td>Puck spinning, wire, hose, pulse reading on the I/O page</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cars not triggering the wash, yellow light stuck on</td><td class=\"k\">Photo eyes</td><td>Eyes lined up, I/O input, water in the conduit box, small breakers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sagging brushes, weak soap, chain off the sprocket, rollers won't come up</td><td class=\"k\">Air</td><td>Compressors at 125 PSI and running, air leaks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chain stopped</td><td class=\"k\">Conveyor</td><td>Roller jams, E-stops, anti-collision, pulse, drive VFD or hydraulics</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Brush not moving in or out</td><td class=\"k\">Brush air</td><td>Air at the compressor and brush assembly, leaks at the cylinder</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Brush not spinning</td><td class=\"k\">Brush drive</td><td>MCC alerts, VFD letters, hydraulic leaks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Weak blower air</td><td class=\"k\">Blowers</td><td>Power lock opening, trash in the net, air pressure</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Low vacuum suction</td><td class=\"k\">Vacuums</td><td>One or two bays: clog. All bays: socks, canister screen, debris</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Low prep pressure or leaks</td><td class=\"k\">Prep unit</td><td>Chemical filter, water in the tank, nozzle, swivel, hoses</td></tr></table>","quiz":[{"q":"The wash stops. Where do you look first?","o":["The vacuums","The MCC alerts, history and I/O pages","The pay station","The blower nets"],"a":1,"why":"MCC first."},{"q":"Rollers won't come up and the soap is weak. Which system do you check?","o":["Air","Pulse","Vacuums","Prep unit"],"a":0,"why":"Both run on air."},{"q":"Only some vacuum bays are weak. What is most likely?","o":["The turbine","The socks in every canister","A clog in those bays' lines","The blowers"],"a":2,"why":"One or two bays is a clog; all bays is the canisters."},{"q":"It is past a site-level fix. What do you give the service tech?","o":["The car count","Nothing","Just the site name","The alert, what you saw, what you checked and tried, and since when"],"a":3,"why":"A clear call is a faster fix."},{"q":"A brush will not spin and the VFD shows letters. What does that mean?","o":["The VFD has an error","Everything is fine","The air is low","The pulse failed"],"a":0,"why":"Letters on a VFD mean an error."}]},{"key":"t2-3","title":"Hoses and Fittings","minutes":5,"why":"A busted hose or a leaking swivel can take down prep, a brush or a chemical line. A replacement that is the wrong size leaks again or fails sooner, so recognizing sizes and matching exactly is the whole skill.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Tier 2 means recognizing the hose sizes on your site's equipment and changing hoses and fittings so they hold.</p>\n<h2>Recognizing hose sizes</h2>\n<ul><li>Learn the sizes used on your site's equipment: prep, brushes and chemical lines.</li><li>Read the markings printed along the hose.</li><li>A replacement matches the one you removed: the same size, the same fittings and the same length.</li></ul>\n<h2>A busted hose</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Lock out the equipment.</li><li>Remove the hose.</li><li>Take it to the repair shop and get one made exactly like the one you took off.</li><li>Install it, then check for leaks under pressure before you leave it.</li></ol>\n<h2>A leak on the prep gun swivel</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Remove the swivel and replace it, using two crescent wrenches.</li><li>Re-Teflon the fitting.</li></ol>\n<h2>Fittings and leaks to watch</h2>\n<ul><li>Change brush fittings when needed.</li><li>Look for leaks around the brush air cylinders.</li><li>Look for leaks between the prep pump and the gun, and on the hoses and unit.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"A hose busts. What is the standard?","o":["Patch it with tape","Remove it, take it to the repair shop and get one made exactly like it","Use any hose on the shelf","Leave it for the service tech"],"a":1,"why":"Exactly like the one you took off."},{"q":"A replacement hose must match the old one in ...","o":["color only","length only","size, fittings and length","brand only"],"a":2,"why":"Same size, same fittings, same length."},{"q":"The prep gun swivel leaks. What do you do?","o":["Remove and replace it with two crescents, and re-Teflon the fitting","Tighten it with pliers","Replace the pump","Call the chemical vendor"],"a":0,"why":"Swivel out, swivel in, Teflon on the fitting."},{"q":"After installing a hose, what do you check before leaving it?","o":["The car count","Nothing","The vacuum socks","For leaks under pressure"],"a":3,"why":"A fix is not done until it holds."}]},{"key":"t2-4","title":"Minor Electrical","minutes":5,"why":"A tripped breaker or a VFD fault is one of the most common reasons a piece of equipment stops, and one of the fastest to clear if you know what you are looking at. Knowing where minor electrical ends is just as important.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Minor electrical at site level means finding and resetting: breakers and VFDs. It does not mean repairing wiring or motors.</p>\n<h2>Breakers</h2>\n<ul><li>Tripped breakers in the MCC can cause a machine to malfunction. Find the tripped one.</li><li>Small breakers in the MCC power the photo eyes.</li><li>The hydraulic motor and the vacuum turbine have breakers that can trip.</li><li>One reset. If it trips again, call the service tech.</li></ul>\n<h2>VFDs</h2>\n<table><tr><th>The VFD shows</th><th>Meaning and action</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"v\">0000</td><td>Working correctly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"v\">Letters</td><td>An error. Check the MCC alerts and note the code for the service tech.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"v\">P5</td><td>Turn the power off to the VFD, let it clear, then power it back on.</td></tr></table>\n<h2>Water and electricity</h2>\n<p>When the photo eyes have no lights, open the conduit box and check for water. Water in electrical is a service tech call.</p>\n<div class=\"callout\"><div class=\"eyebrow red\">Where minor electrical ends</div><p>Anything past a reset is the service tech: a breaker that trips again, wiring, motors, or water in electrical.</p></div>","quiz":[{"q":"A conveyor VFD shows P5. What do you do?","o":["Hit reset until it clears","Turn power off to the VFD, let it clear, then power it back on","Replace the VFD","Ignore it"],"a":1,"why":"That is the P5 clear."},{"q":"What does 0000 on a VFD mean?","o":["An error","P5","It is working correctly","It is off"],"a":2,"why":"0000 is working."},{"q":"A breaker trips a second time after one reset. What now?","o":["Reset it again","Call the service tech","Swap it with another breaker","Tape it"],"a":1,"why":"One reset."},{"q":"There is water in the photo eye conduit box. Whose fix is that?","o":["The service tech's","Anyone on the crew","Nobody; it will dry","The customer's"],"a":0,"why":"Water in electrical is past site level."}]},{"key":"t2-5","title":"Changing Oils and Greasing","minutes":5,"why":"Oil and grease are the cheapest maintenance there is. A compressor or pump that runs on old oil, or a bearing that runs dry, fails early and usually on a busy day.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Changing oils and greasing are routine Tier 2 work. Lock out first, and use the oil and grease the equipment manual calls for.</p>\n<h2>Oils</h2>\n<table><tr><th>Equipment</th><th>What</th><th>How often</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Air compressors</td><td>Change the oil. Look for oil around the pumps.</td><td>Monthly</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Prep pump</td><td>Change the pump oil. Check for oil leaks.</td><td>Monthly (leaks weekly)</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Hydraulic packs</td><td>Keep them filled with oil. Look for spills around the area.</td><td>On every check</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Air water separators</td><td>Drain them and add oil.</td><td>Regularly</td></tr></table>\n<h2>Grease</h2>\n<ul><li>Brush bearings and cylinder shafts</li><li>Conveyor take-up bearings</li><li>Blower power locks</li><li>Vacuum turbine bearings</li></ul>\n<p>When an oil change or grease point is done, it gets written down. A maintenance task that is not written down did not happen.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"How often is the prep pump oil changed?","o":["Monthly","Daily","Yearly","Never"],"a":0,"why":"Monthly, the same as the air compressors."},{"q":"Which of these get greased?","o":["Brush bearings and cylinder shafts","Conveyor take-up bearings","Blower power locks","All of these"],"a":3,"why":"All grease points."},{"q":"What do you do with the air water separators?","o":["Nothing","Replace them monthly","Drain them and add oil","Grease them"],"a":2,"why":"Drain and add oil."},{"q":"Which oil or grease do you use?","o":["Whatever is on the shelf","What the equipment manual calls for","The cheapest","Motor oil for everything"],"a":1,"why":"Follow the manual."}]},{"key":"t2-6","title":"The Conveyor Chain and Rollers","minutes":5,"why":"A chain that keeps slipping off the sprocket shuts the wash down again and again. Knowing what the tension cylinders are telling you, and how to take up a loose chain, keeps the backbone running.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">The chain tension cylinders tell you the state of the chain. Read them before you do anything else.</p>\n<table><tr><th>The cylinders are</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Action</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Fully extended</td><td>The chain is loose.</td><td>Remove a link. Then record the removed link in the RTC.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Fully collapsed</td><td>Not enough tension air.</td><td>Check the air tension pressure (90 PSI) and the air compressors.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">In between, at 90 PSI</td><td>Correct.</td><td>Nothing. Keep watching it.</td></tr></table>\n<h2>Site-level conveyor work</h2>\n<ul><li>Lock out the conveyor first.</li><li>Remove a chain link when the chain is loose, and record it in the RTC every time.</li><li>Change a roller that is damaged or twisted.</li><li>Grease the take-up bearings.</li></ul>\n<h2>The drive</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Electric:</strong> check the VFD in the MCC. It reads 0000 when working. On P5, power the VFD off, let it clear, and power it back on.</li><li><strong>Hydraulic:</strong> look for oil spills, check the packs are filled, and check the hydraulic motor breaker.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"The tension cylinders are fully extended. What do you do?","o":["Add air","Remove a link and record it in the RTC","Replace the sprocket","Nothing"],"a":1,"why":"Fully extended is a loose chain."},{"q":"The tension cylinders are fully collapsed. What do you check?","o":["The vac socks","The prep tank","The air tension pressure and the air compressors","The photo eyes"],"a":2,"why":"Collapsed means not enough tension air."},{"q":"What must happen every time a chain link is removed?","o":["Nothing","It is recorded in the RTC","The conveyor is replaced","The site closes"],"a":1,"why":"Every removed link gets recorded."},{"q":"Before removing a link or changing a roller, you ...","o":["lock out the conveyor","slow it down","wash one more car","call corporate"],"a":0,"why":"Lockout first."}]},{"key":"t2-7","title":"The Site Maintenance Schedule","minutes":5,"why":"Most breakdowns start as skipped routine maintenance. A site that does its daily, weekly and monthly items on time has fewer stops, and the ones it has are easier to find.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">This is every site-level maintenance item from the systems, in one schedule.</p>\n<table><tr><th>How often</th><th>Item</th></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Daily</td><td>Drain water from the air compressors. Vacuums: shake the socks and clear the bin, wipe down the seals, clear the canister screen.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Weekly</td><td>Air compressors: check the air filters and the belt tension. Prep unit: check the belt tension, clean around the area, check for oil leaks. Vacuums: lube the hinges.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Monthly</td><td>Change the air compressor oil. Change the prep pump oil.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Every 3 months</td><td>Remove the vac socks, pressure wash them and let them dry.</td></tr>\n<tr><td class=\"k\">Regularly and as needed</td><td>Grease the brush bearings, cylinder shafts, take-up bearings, blower power locks and turbine bearings. Drain and oil the air water separators. Clear the blower nets and wipe the housings. Change brush fittings. Remove a chain link when the chain is loose. Change rollers. Change the prep gun when it leaks from the handle or stops working.</td></tr></table>\n<p>Each item gets done, and each one gets written down.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"Which is a daily item?","o":["Change the compressor oil","Pressure wash the vac socks","Drain the air compressor water","Lube the vacuum hinges"],"a":2,"why":"Draining compressor water is daily."},{"q":"Which is a weekly item?","o":["Check compressor air filters and belt tension","Change the prep pump oil","Pressure wash the vac socks","Drain compressor water"],"a":0,"why":"Filters and belts are weekly."},{"q":"Which are monthly items?","o":["Clearing the canister screen","Shaking the vac socks","Lubing the hinges","Changing the air compressor oil and the prep pump oil"],"a":3,"why":"Both oil changes are monthly."},{"q":"The prep gun is leaking from the handle. What do you do?","o":["Tape it","Change the prep gun","Turn the pressure down","Ignore it"],"a":1,"why":"A gun that leaks from the handle gets changed."}]}]}],"scenarios":[{"t":"The returning trip","s":"You reset a tripped breaker for the turbine. Twenty minutes later it trips again.","o":["Reset it again","Leave it off, note the time and what you saw, and call the service tech","Swap it with another breaker","Tape the breaker on"],"a":1,"why":"One reset. A second trip is a failing part, not a fluke."},{"t":"The quick swivel","s":"The prep gun swivel is spraying. A crew member grabs a pair of pliers to tighten it while the pump runs.","o":["Let them; it is quick","Stop them, lock out the prep unit, replace the swivel with two crescents and re-Teflon the fitting","Turn the pressure up","Tape the swivel"],"a":1,"why":"Lockout first, the right tools, and a real fix."},{"t":"The close-enough hose","s":"A brush hose busts. There is a hose on the shelf that is about the same length but a different size.","o":["Use it for now","Use it and order the right one later","Lock out, remove the busted hose and take it to the repair shop to have one made exactly like it","Run the brush without the hose"],"a":2,"why":"Matching the hose exactly is the standard. A wrong size leaks again."},{"t":"The loose chain","s":"The chain came off the sprocket twice this morning. Air is at 125 PSI, chain tension is at 90 PSI, and the tension cylinders are fully extended.","o":["Lock out, remove a link, record it in the RTC, then watch the chain","Turn the tension air up","Keep putting it back on","Replace the sprocket"],"a":0,"why":"Fully extended cylinders with good air mean the chain is loose. A link comes out, and it gets recorded."},{"t":"P5 on the drive","s":"The conveyor stopped. The conveyor VFD in the MCC shows P5.","o":["Replace the VFD","Call corporate","Hit every button until it runs","Turn off the power to the VFD, let it clear, and power it back on"],"a":3,"why":"That is the P5 clear. If it comes back, call it in."}],"assessment":[{"q":"Before hands-on work you ...","o":["grab the wrench","stop, disconnect at the equipment, tag and verify","tell the crew to keep washing","call the tech"],"a":1},{"q":"A fault returns right after one reset. You ...","o":["reset again","stop resetting and call it in","ignore it","tape the breaker"],"a":1},{"q":"A replacement hose must match the old one in ...","o":["size, fittings and length","color","brand","price"],"a":0},{"q":"A busted hose is ...","o":["taped","replaced with one made exactly like it at the repair shop","left until closing","swapped for the nearest hose"],"a":1},{"q":"A swivel is replaced with ...","o":["pliers","one wrench","two crescent wrenches, and the fitting re-Tefloned","bare hands"],"a":2},{"q":"A VFD showing P5 is cleared by ...","o":["powering the VFD off, letting it clear, and powering back on","hitting reset repeatedly","replacing it","unplugging the MCC"],"a":0},{"q":"Letters on a VFD mean ...","o":["it is working","an error","P5 only","low air"],"a":1},{"q":"Air compressor oil and prep pump oil are changed ...","o":["weekly","monthly","every 3 months","yearly"],"a":1},{"q":"Tension cylinders fully extended means you ...","o":["add air","remove a link and record it in the RTC","replace the chain","do nothing"],"a":1},{"q":"Tension cylinders fully collapsed means you check ...","o":["the vacuum socks","the photo eyes","the air tension pressure and the compressors","the pulse"],"a":2},{"q":"Air water separators are ...","o":["drained and refilled with oil","greased","replaced weekly","left alone"],"a":0},{"q":"Water in the photo eye conduit box is ...","o":["dried with a towel and ignored","a service tech call","normal","fixed by resetting the MCC"],"a":1}]},{"key":"t3","num":3,"title":"Tier 3: Maintenance Trainer","short":"T3 Maintenance","draft":true,"pass":80,"role":"Tier 3 holds Tier 1 and Tier 2, and can teach the items and talk team members through them, on the lot or over the phone.","blurb":"Teaching the systems, talking someone through a fix, running fire drills, and owning the site maintenance schedule.","why":"One leader who can fix the wash helps one shift. A leader who can teach it makes every shift capable. Tier 3 is how maintenance knowledge spreads past the person who happens to be on site.","ack_statement":"I hold Tier 1 and Tier 2 Maintenance, and I can teach them. I can show a team member each system and the checks that go with it, talk them through a site-level fix without doing it for them, run the fire drills with the crew, and keep the site maintenance schedule on track.","skills":["Taught a team member one system from Tier 1: what it does, where it is, how it works, and its first checks","Talked a team member through a Tier 2 fix without doing it for them","Ran a \"wash goes down\" fire drill with the crew","Ran a \"brush goes down\" drill, including tying a brush back","Walked a team member through the daily and weekly site maintenance items"],"requires":["t2"],"courses":[{"title":"Maintenance Trainer","lessons":[{"key":"t3-1","title":"Teaching the Systems","minutes":5,"why":"People learn equipment by standing at it. A list read in the office is forgotten by the next shift; a system shown, done and checked at the machine sticks.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Tier 3 means teaching the items, not just doing them. Show, do not just tell.</p>\n<h2>Teach at the equipment</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><strong>Show it.</strong> Stand at the system. Point out what it does, where it is, and what working correctly looks like.</li><li><strong>Explain why.</strong> Use the body: the MCC is the brain, the pulse the heart, the compressors the lungs, the conveyor the backbone, the brushes the hands and feet. People remember the picture.</li><li><strong>Let them do it.</strong> They read the MCC page, find the input, walk the track. You watch.</li><li><strong>Check it.</strong> Have them explain it back to you, in their own words, including the first checks and when to call the service tech.</li></ol>\n<h2>One system at a time</h2>\n<p>Teach one system well, then the next. Start with what stops the wash most at your site.</p>\n<h2>Safety is taught first</h2>\n<p>Before you teach any hands-on item, teach lockout. The person you train will copy what you do, including the shortcuts.</p>","quiz":[{"q":"Where is maintenance best taught?","o":["At the equipment","In the office","By text","From the manual only"],"a":0,"why":"People learn equipment by standing at it."},{"q":"How do you check that the lesson stuck?","o":["Ask \"got it?\"","Have them explain it back in their own words, including the first checks","Hand them the manual","Assume it did"],"a":1,"why":"Explaining it back shows what they actually understood."},{"q":"What is taught before any hands-on item?","o":["The car count","Lockout","Pricing","The schedule"],"a":1,"why":"Lockout first, and they copy what you do."},{"q":"Which system do you teach first?","o":["Whichever is easiest","The vacuums always","The one that stops the wash most at your site","None; let them learn on their own"],"a":2,"why":"Start where it matters most."}]},{"key":"t3-2","title":"Talking Someone Through a Fix","minutes":5,"why":"You won't always be on site when the wash goes down. A leader who can talk a team member through the checks over the phone gets the wash back up without the drive, and teaches at the same time.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Talking a team member through an item means they do it and you guide. You don't take over, and you don't skip steps.</p>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li><strong>Safety first.</strong> Make sure the equipment is stopped, and locked out before any hands go on it.</li><li><strong>Ask what they see.</strong> Have them read you the MCC alert and what the equipment is doing.</li><li><strong>One step at a time.</strong> Give one check, wait for what they see, then give the next.</li><li><strong>Have them describe, not decide.</strong> \"Is the puck spinning?\" gets a better answer than \"Is the pulse okay?\"</li><li><strong>Confirm the result.</strong> Have them run a test and tell you what happened.</li><li><strong>Know when to stop.</strong> If it is past site level, stop and call the service tech with what you both found.</li><li><strong>Write it down.</strong> What failed, what was done, by whom, and when.</li></ol>","quiz":[{"q":"When talking someone through a fix, what comes first?","o":["The fastest fix","Making sure the equipment is stopped and locked out","Asking about the car count","Calling corporate"],"a":1,"why":"Safety first, even on the phone."},{"q":"Which question gets a better answer?","o":["\"Is the pulse okay?\"","\"Is the puck spinning?\"","\"Did you fix it?\"","\"Is everything fine?\""],"a":1,"why":"Ask them to describe what they see."},{"q":"How many steps do you give at once?","o":["All of them","One, then wait for what they see","None","Three"],"a":1,"why":"One step at a time."},{"q":"The problem is past site level. What do you do?","o":["Keep trying things","Have them reset it repeatedly","Stop and call the service tech with what you both found","Close the wash and go home"],"a":2,"why":"Know when to stop."}]},{"key":"t3-3","title":"Running Fire Drills","minutes":4,"why":"A crew that has practiced a breakdown handles a real one calmly. The first time the crew sees the wash go down should not be on a busy Saturday.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">A fire drill is practice for the moment the wash or a brush goes down. Tier 3 runs them, so the crew knows the order before they need it.</p>\n<h2>The wash goes down</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Find the issue.</li><li>Inform the crew.</li><li>Direct the crew.</li><li>Start the corrective operation if possible.</li><li>Call for service tech help.</li></ol>\n<h2>A brush goes down</h2>\n<ol class=\"steps\"><li>Find the issue.</li><li>Troubleshoot it.</li><li>Inform the service tech.</li><li>Tie the brush back if needed.</li></ol>\n<h2>Running the drill</h2>\n<ul><li>Run it during a slow time, never with cars in the tunnel.</li><li>Walk the crew through who does what, then have them do it.</li><li>Practice tying back each brush at your site, the way your service tech showed you.</li><li>Afterward, talk through what was slow or unclear.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"When do you run a fire drill?","o":["During a slow time, with no cars in the tunnel","During the Saturday rush","Never; wait for the real thing","At closing with cars in the tunnel"],"a":0,"why":"Practice when it is safe."},{"q":"In the \"brush goes down\" drill, what does the crew practice?","o":["Washing through it","Tying the brush back","Removing the brush","Restarting the MCC"],"a":1,"why":"Tie-back is part of the brush drill."},{"q":"What happens after a drill?","o":["Nothing","Talk through what was slow or unclear","Write everyone up","Run it again with cars"],"a":1,"why":"The debrief is where the drill pays off."}]},{"key":"t3-4","title":"Owning the Site Maintenance","minutes":4,"why":"At Tier 3 the job is not just doing maintenance, it is making sure it happens on every shift, catching patterns before they become breakdowns, and building the next person who can do it.","body":"<p class=\"lead\">Tier 3 owns the site's maintenance: the schedule, the patterns and the people.</p>\n<h2>The schedule</h2>\n<ul><li>Daily, weekly and monthly items are assigned, done and written down.</li><li>Walk the site and check the work, not just the log.</li></ul>\n<h2>The patterns</h2>\n<ul><li>A fault that returns is a failing part. Repeated resets hide it until it fails for good.</li><li>A chain that keeps needing links removed, a breaker that keeps tripping, or a hose that keeps busting is a call to the service tech, with the history.</li></ul>\n<h2>The people</h2>\n<ul><li>Get the site's leaders to Tier 1, then Tier 2.</li><li>A General Manager needs Tier 1 to be certified.</li><li>The site should never depend on one person who knows the equipment.</li></ul>","quiz":[{"q":"A breaker at your site has tripped three times this week. What is it?","o":["Bad luck","A pattern: a failing part to call in with the history","Normal","The crew's fault"],"a":1,"why":"A returning fault is a failing part."},{"q":"How do you know the schedule is actually being done?","o":["Trust the log","Walk the site and check the work, not just the log","Ask once a year","You can't"],"a":1,"why":"Check the work."},{"q":"What does a General Manager need to be certified?","o":["Tier 3","Tier 2","Tier 1","No maintenance tier"],"a":2,"why":"Tier 1 is required for GM certification."}]}]}],"scenarios":[{"t":"The phone call","s":"Your Supervisor calls: the wash stopped and they \"checked everything.\" You are 40 minutes away.","o":["Drive over right away and fix it yourself","Tell them to keep resetting","Ask what the MCC alert says, then walk them through the checks one at a time, safety first","Tell them to close for the day"],"a":2,"why":"Talk them through it: their eyes, your knowledge, one step at a time."},{"t":"The quick learner","s":"A new Supervisor says they already understand the air compressors after you described them in the office.","o":["Take them to the compressors, show them, have them do the checks and explain them back","Take their word for it","Give them the manual","Move on to the next system"],"a":0,"why":"Show, do, check. Understanding is shown at the equipment."},{"t":"The skipped drill","s":"Your AGM says fire drills are a waste of time because \"we'll figure it out when it happens.\"","o":["Agree and skip them","Run them only after a breakdown","Explain that a practiced crew handles a real breakdown calmly, and run the drill at a slow time","Do it on a busy Saturday for realism"],"a":2,"why":"Practice when it is safe, so the real one is short."},{"t":"The third hose","s":"The same prep hose has busted three times in a month. Each time it was replaced correctly.","o":["Keep replacing it","Treat it as a pattern: call the service tech with the history","Stop using prep","Use a bigger hose"],"a":1,"why":"A repeat failure is a pattern. The history is what gets it fixed for good."}],"assessment":[{"q":"The teaching order at the equipment is ...","o":["tell, then test","show it, explain why, let them do it, check it","hand them the manual","do it for them"],"a":1},{"q":"Before teaching any hands-on item you teach ...","o":["pricing","lockout","the car count","nothing"],"a":1},{"q":"Talking someone through a fix, you give ...","o":["every step at once","one step at a time and wait for what they see","the answer only","nothing; they figure it out"],"a":1},{"q":"You ask them to ...","o":["describe what they see","decide if it is fine","reset it repeatedly","skip the MCC"],"a":0},{"q":"Fire drills are run ...","o":["during a slow time with no cars in the tunnel","during the rush","never","only by the service tech"],"a":0},{"q":"The brush-down drill includes ...","o":["tying the brush back","removing the brush","washing through it","changing the VFD"],"a":0},{"q":"A fault that keeps returning is ...","o":["bad luck","a failing part to call in with the history","normal","fixed by resetting"],"a":1},{"q":"You check the maintenance schedule by ...","o":["trusting the log","walking the site and checking the work","asking the crew","waiting for a breakdown"],"a":1},{"q":"A General Manager is certified only once they hold ...","o":["Tier 1","Tier 2","Tier 3","no tier"],"a":0},{"q":"Past site level, the call to the service tech includes ...","o":["what you found, what you checked and tried, and since when","only the site name","nothing","the car count"],"a":0}]}],"prereqs":[{"level":"l6","requires":"t1"}]}$academy$::jsonb);
